          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            By a
            %em braid group
            \&amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#Artin25"}> Artin (1925)
            \&amp;rbrack; one means the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            of joint continuous
            %em motions
            \&amp;lbrack;cf.
            = succeed "&rbrack;" do
              %a{:href => "#Goldsmith81"} Goldsmith (1981)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n+1
            of non-coincident points in the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/plane"} plane
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+space+of+points"} configuration
            back to that fixed configuration. The “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldlines"}> worldlines
            ” traced out by such points in space-time under such an operation look like a braid with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n+1
            strands, whence the name.
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GenericBraidGroupElement-230205.jpg", :width => "280"}/
          %p
            As with actual braids, here it is understood that two such operations are identified if they differ only by continuous deformations of the “strands” without breaking or intersecting these, hence that one identifies those such systems of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldlines"} worldlines
            which are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isotopy"} isotopic
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^3
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/link+diagrams"} link diagrams
            — such as under
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reidemeister+moves"} Reidemeister moves
            — and in fact every link diagram may be obtained by “closing up” a braid diagram, in the evident sense.)
          %p
            A quick way of saying this with precision is to observe that a braid group is thus the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+space+of+points"} configuration space of points
            in the plane (see
            %a{:href => "#AsFundamentalGroupOfConfigurationSpace"} below
            for more).
          %p Here it makes a key difference whether:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                one considers the points in a configuration as
                %em ordered
                (labeled by numbers
                = succeed ")" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mi ⋯
                        %mo ,
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1, \cdots, n+1
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+braid+group"} pure braid group
            %li
              %p
                or as indistinguishable (albeit in any case with distinct positions!) in which case one speaks of the
                %em braid group
                proper.
          %p
            Namely, after traveling along a general braid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            the order of the given points may come out permuted by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/permutation"} permutation
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} perm
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{perm}(b)
            %em pure
            precisely if this permutation is trivial:
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/BraidGroupFibration-230205.jpg", :width => "600"}/
          %p
            More explicitly, braid groups admit
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+presented+group"} finite presentations
            by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generators+and+relations"} generators and relations
          %p
            %br/
          %h2#DefinitionsAndCharacterizations Definitions and characterizations
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#ByGeneratorsAndRelations"} Via generators and relations
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#AsFundamentalGroupOfConfigurationSpace"} As the fundamental group of a configuration space of points
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#BraidGroupsAsMappingClassGroupsOfPuncturedSurfaces"} As mapping class group of punctured surfaces
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#AsAutomorphismsOfAFreeGroup"} As automorphisms of a free group
          %h3#ByGeneratorsAndRelations Via generators and relations
          %h4#PresentationOfGeneralBraids Presentation of general braids
          %p
            We discuss the braid group as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+generated+group"} finitely generated group
            (
            %a{:href => "#Artin25"}> Artin 1925, (5)-(6)
            ;
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#Artin47"} Artin 1947, (18)-(19)
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#FoxNeuwirth62"} Fox &amp; Neuwirth 1962, §7
          %p
          #ArtinPresentation.num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              %strong (Artin presentation)
            %p
              The
              = succeed "," do
                %strong Artin braid group
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Br
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Br({n+1})
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n+1
              strands is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+generated+group"} finitely generated group
              given via
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generators+and+relations"} generators and relations
